<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Baudelaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles-Pierre Baudelaire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Baudelaire was a French poet, essayist, translator and art critic, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His collection Les Fleurs du mal (The Flowers of Evil), published in 1857, dealt with sex, death and the changing city; he, his publisher and the printer were prosecuted for an offence against public morals, six poems were suppressed, and the judgment was reversed only in 1949. He translated stories by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-allan-poe">Edgar Allan Poe</a>, coined the term modernité for the fleeting experience of urban life, and influenced Paul Verlaine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-rimbaud">Arthur Rimbaud</a> and Stéphane Mallarmé. After a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1866 he was left paralysed and died in Paris in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-baudelaire.jpg" alt="Charles Baudelaire" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Notwist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baudelaire_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
